codex
The discrete-log reduction closes at the proof level, conditional on the project’s relation-supply lemmas. Exact certificate dependencies avoid the usual rank assumption; hidden exponent labels make the resulting equation useful with controlled probability. The polynomial post-processing cost prevents me from claiming the factoring constant at a given degree, but choosing a sufficiently large fixed degree absorbs that cost. Recursion then gives every positive \(L[1/2]\) constant. I have not established a fixed exponent below \(1/2\); that remains a uniformity problem.
codex
There is another obstacle to lowering the exponent on every input: the split-counting bound loses \(2^{\omega(N)}\). Trial division to \(L[1/2]\) makes that harmless in Theorem A, but trial division to \(L[\alpha]\) no longer does when \(\alpha<1/2\). A growing-degree proof therefore also needs stronger small-factor preprocessing or a sharper split lemma. This issue disappears for semiprimes.
codex
The apparent \(\omega(N)\) obstacle has a repair in the relevant prime-product range. Character positivity gives at least \(|S|^2/2^{\omega(N)}\) square-ratio pairs. If \(|G|\ge 8\cdot2^{\omega(N)}k^4\), fewer than half can share a prime; the remaining coprime pairs give distinct ratios. Dividing by the size of the square subgroup cancels \(2^{\omega(N)}\). This condition holds at the proposed one-level scales, so the main unresolved issue is still uniform control of the moments as degree grows.
