The two strongest findings are the recursive improvement and a prime-field DL transfer that avoids the usual NFS rank assumption.

**1. Recursive factoring: the fixed-point calculation survives scrutiny.**

The published comparison is Lenstra–Pomerance, “A rigorous time bound for factoring integers,” *JAMS* **5** (1992), 483–516, giving expected \(L_N[1/2,1]\) complete factorization. [Original paper](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/rigorous.pdf).

The source chain proves its moment estimates for arbitrary fixed \(a>0\), before the final choice \(a=\theta/\sqrt2\). That freedom is essential: keeping the old choice of \(a\) does not give the optimized recursion.

Suppose the shape test uses a factoring algorithm of constant \(C\). Since its two inputs are at most \(\sqrt{2N}\), its cost is
\[
L_N^{C/\sqrt2+o(1)}.
\]
The parent’s exponent is therefore
\[
\max\left\{
a+\frac C{\sqrt2},\
a+\frac{\theta}{2a},\
2a
\right\}.                                                    \tag{1}
\]
The batch-smoothness \(B\) term is already covered by \(2a\). This is the ledger in [Theorem A, Step 10](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmA_proof.md:309), with its shape-test entry replaced.

At a recursive fixed point, put
\[
a=\left(1-\frac1{\sqrt2}\right)C.
\]
Equating the two relation terms gives
\[
C=a+\frac{\theta}{2a},
\qquad
\boxed{C_\theta^2=(1+\sqrt2)\theta}.
\]
Moreover,
\[
2a=(2-\sqrt2)C<C,
\]
so linear algebra does not obstruct this solution.

For an actual theorem, choose \(C>C_\theta\) and retain positive slack in every inequality. For the maximum expected cost on integers up to \(N\), the resulting recurrence has the form
\[
T(N)\le
L_N^{a+\varepsilon}\,T(\sqrt{2N})
+
L_N^{a+\theta/(2a)+\varepsilon}
+
L_N^{2a+\varepsilon},
\]
up to polynomial factors in \(\log N\). Strong induction closes it. Complete factorization adds only \(O(\log N)\) splitting calls. Recursive shape-test coins depend on \(u,v\), hence on \(R\), and preserve transcript invariance.

Some finite-parameter checks, before adding positive slack:

| \(d,k\) | Optimized \(\theta\) | Recursive threshold \(C_\theta\) |
|---|---:|---:|
| \(9,13\) | \(16/39\) | \(0.995212\) |
| \(9,1000\) | \(0.30085\) | \(0.852242\) |
| **\(15,1000\)** | **\(0.18840625\)** | **\(0.674428\)** |
| \(31,1000\) | \(0.094703125\) | \(0.478156\) |

Thus the \(1/\sqrt2\) floor is genuinely removed. The recursive formula was already anticipated in [hypD](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/hypD_results.md:104); the later supply results make its use substantive.

Because
\[
\inf_{k,\eta_0}\theta=\frac3{d+1},
\]
the recursive constants tend to zero as fixed odd \(d\) increases. Consequently:

> **PROVED from the internal chain:** for every \(c>0\), some fixed-parameter Las Vegas algorithm completely factors every \(N\) in expected \(L_N[1/2,c]\) time.

There is also a single-algorithm consequence. Dovetail all validated fixed-parameter algorithms with fixed positive time shares. For each \(c>0\), one component supplies that bound; its fixed scheduling overhead does not affect the exponent. This yields
\[
\boxed{\mathbb E T(N)=\exp(o(\Lambda_N)).}
\]
This portfolio argument needs no uniform growing-degree estimate. It supplies **no specified rate** for the \(o(\Lambda_N)\), however, and therefore proves no \(L[\alpha]\) bound with fixed \(\alpha<1/2\).

**Missing lemma:** none beyond A’s internal ingredients; the remaining task is a formal recursive cost lemma.

**First step:** write that lemma using the maximum expected complete-factorization cost up to \(N\), positive exponent slack, and the existing absorbing-factor-exit bookkeeping. This should precede growing-degree work.

